     "explanation": "The limbic brain governs feelings such as trust and loyalty and drives much of our decisive behavior, but it has no capacity for language. The neocortex, which handles language and rational analysis, often steps in afterward to construct a plausible explanation — a process called confabulation. That is why 'it just felt right' is often the most honest answer someone can give.",
     "visual": {
      "type": "diagram",
      "svg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 1100 1100\"><rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"1100\" height=\"1100\" fill=\"#1a1a2e\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"80\" font-size=\"30\" fill=\"#ffffff\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Which Brain Has Words?</text><ellipse cx=\"550\" cy=\"420\" rx=\"320\" ry=\"260\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#888\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"200\" font-size=\"20\" fill=\"#888\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Neocortex</text><ellipse cx=\"550\" cy=\"430\" rx=\"150\" ry=\"120\" fill=\"#ec4899\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"420\" font-size=\"20\" fill=\"#ffffff\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Limbic</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"450\" font-size=\"18\" fill=\"#ffffff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">no language</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"250\" font-size=\"18\" fill=\"#ffffff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">language + logic</text><text x=\"300\" y=\"750\" font-size=\"18\" fill=\"#ffd700\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Feeling comes first...</text><text x=\"800\" y=\"750\" font-size=\"18\" fill=\"#ffd700\" text-anchor=\"middle\">...words come second</text><line x1=\"420\" y1=\"770\" x2=\"680\" y2=\"770\" stroke=\"#ffd700\" stroke-width=\"3\" marker-end=\"url(#arrowq)\"/><defs><marker id=\"arrowq\" markerWidth=\"10\" markerHeight=\"10\" refX=\"8\" refY=\"5\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L10,5 L0,10 z\" fill=\"#ffd700\"/></marker></defs><text x=\"550\" y=\"950\" font-size=\"18\" fill=\"#10b981\" text-anchor=\"middle\">The limbic brain: powerful behavior, zero vocabulary</text></svg>",
      "caption": "The limbic brain drives feeling and behavior but has no words; the neocortex supplies language after the decision is already made."
     }
